---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const psy = await getCollection('en_psychology');
const bio = await getCollection('en_biology');
const all = [...psy.map((p) => ({...p, cat: 'psychology' as const})), ...bio.map((p) => ({...p, cat: 'biology' as const}))];
const relatedTags = ['aging', 'longevity', 'telomere', 'mitochondria', 'autophagy', 'senescence'];
const related = all
  .filter((p) => (p.data.tags ?? []).some((t) => relatedTags.includes(t)))
  .sort((a, b) => (a.data.generated_at < b.data.generated_at ? 1 : -1))
  .slice(0, 20);

export const prerender = true;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Longevity Research Hub: Aging Biology, Telomeres, Mitochondria｜PubMed Trivia"
  description="Curated evidence on human longevity — telomere biology, mitochondrial function, cellular senescence, autophagy, and lifestyle factors associated with extended healthspan. Based on peer-reviewed systematic reviews."
  lang="en"
>
  <article class="hub">
    <header class="hub__header">
      <p class="hub__breadcrumb"><a href="/en/">Home</a> › 🧬 Longevity Hub</p>
      <h1>🧬 Longevity Research: An Evidence Hub</h1>
      <p class="hub__lede">
        Longevity science has transformed from fringe speculation to a major translational research field.
        This hub curates <strong>peer-reviewed systematic reviews</strong> on the biology of aging —
        telomere dynamics, mitochondrial decline, cellular senescence, autophagy, and the lifestyle
        factors most strongly associated with extended healthspan.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="hub__toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <p class="hub__toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#why-aging">Why we age: current theories</a></li>
        <li><a href="#hallmarks">The hallmarks of aging</a></li>
        <li><a href="#telomeres">Telomeres and cellular aging</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mitochondria">Mitochondria and energy decline</a></li>
        <li><a href="#senescence">Senescence and inflammation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#lifestyle">Lifestyle factors with evidence</a></li>
        <li><a href="#articles">All longevity articles</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="why-aging">
      <h2>1. Why we age: current theories</h2>
      <p>
        Aging is now understood as a <strong>multifactorial biological process</strong>, not a single mechanism.
        The dominant frameworks include <strong>programmed theories</strong> (aging as an evolved endpoint of
        development) and <strong>damage theories</strong> (aging as the accumulated cost of molecular wear).
        Modern research largely integrates both: specific molecular pathways (e.g., mTOR, IGF-1, sirtuins)
        regulate how organisms respond to accumulated damage.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical implication: aging-related decline is not inevitable at any specific rate. Interventions
        that modulate these pathways — caloric restriction, exercise, specific pharmacological agents — can
        extend <em>healthspan</em> (the period of life free from major disease), even without necessarily
        extending maximum lifespan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="hallmarks">
      <h2>2. The hallmarks of aging</h2>
      <p>
        A landmark 2013 review (updated in 2023) identified <strong>twelve hallmarks of aging</strong>,
        each a measurable biological process that worsens with age and whose experimental modulation
        affects aging outcomes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Genomic instability</strong> — accumulated DNA damage</li>
        <li><strong>Telomere attrition</strong> — chromosome end shortening</li>
        <li><strong>Epigenetic alterations</strong> — DNA methylation drift</li>
        <li><strong>Loss of proteostasis</strong> — misfolded protein buildup</li>
        <li><strong>Disabled macroautophagy</strong> — cellular cleanup declining</li>
        <li><strong>Deregulated nutrient sensing</strong> — mTOR, IGF-1, AMPK pathways</li>
        <li><strong>Mitochondrial dysfunction</strong> — energy production decline</li>
        <li><strong>Cellular senescence</strong> — zombie cells accumulating</li>
        <li><strong>Stem cell exhaustion</strong> — tissue repair failing</li>
        <li><strong>Altered intercellular communication</strong> — chronic inflammation</li>
        <li><strong>Chronic inflammation</strong> ("inflammaging")</li>
        <li><strong>Dysbiosis</strong> — gut microbiome degradation</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="telomeres">
      <h2>3. Telomeres and cellular aging</h2>
      <p>
        Telomeres are repetitive DNA sequences capping chromosome ends. They shorten with each cell division,
        and critically short telomeres trigger cellular senescence or death. Research links shorter leukocyte
        telomere length to <strong>cardiovascular disease, cognitive decline, and all-cause mortality</strong>,
        though direction of causation remains debated.
      </p>
      <p>
        Interventions showing modest telomere-protective effects in meta-analyses include regular exercise
        (particularly aerobic), Mediterranean-pattern diets, meditation practices, and reduced chronic stress.
        The size of these effects is small but consistent across studies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mitochondria">
      <h2>4. Mitochondria and energy decline</h2>
      <p>
        Mitochondrial dysfunction is a central hallmark of aging. With age, mitochondrial DNA accumulates
        mutations, membrane integrity declines, and oxidative phosphorylation efficiency drops — producing
        the systemic energy decline characteristic of older organisms.
      </p>
      <p>
        For more detail, see our deep-dive on the <a href="/en/biology/20260921-inner-mitochondrial-membrane-evidence/">inner mitochondrial membrane and cristae structure</a>,
        which synthesizes 10 recent reviews on how mitochondrial architecture supports (or fails to support)
        cellular energy demands throughout the lifespan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="senescence">
      <h2>5. Senescence and inflammation</h2>
      <p>
        Senescent cells accumulate with age — damaged cells that neither divide nor die. They secrete a
        pro-inflammatory profile (the SASP: senescence-associated secretory phenotype) that drives
        <strong>chronic low-grade inflammation</strong> implicated in nearly all age-related diseases.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Senolytic drugs</strong> — compounds that selectively kill senescent cells — are an active
        area of translational research. Early trials in humans (dasatinib + quercetin) show promise for
        specific indications, but general anti-aging claims remain premature.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="lifestyle">
      <h2>6. Lifestyle factors with strongest evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Four interventions have the most robust meta-analytic evidence for extending healthspan in humans:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Regular physical activity</strong> — reduces all-cause mortality 20-30% at moderate doses</li>
        <li><strong>Dietary quality</strong> — Mediterranean and plant-forward patterns reduce cardiovascular mortality</li>
        <li><strong>Social connection</strong> — strong effects on dementia prevention and mental health</li>
        <li><strong>Sleep adequacy</strong> — 7-8 hours associated with lower mortality than &lt;6 or &gt;9</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These effects are additive. People following all four typically show mortality rates 50-70% lower
        than peers following none, even after adjusting for socioeconomic confounders.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="articles">
      <h2>7. All longevity-related articles ({related.length})</h2>
      <ul class="hub__articles">
        {related.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/en/${p.cat}/${p.id}/`}>
              <strong>{p.data.title}</strong>
              <p>{p.data.fact}</p>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="hub__cta"><a href="/en/biology/">→ All Biology articles</a> | <a href="/en/hub/">→ All Hubs</a></p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <style>
    .hub { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px 20px 60px; }
    .hub__breadcrumb { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 8px; }
    .hub__breadcrumb a { color: var(--muted); }
    .hub__header h1 { font-size: 2rem; color: var(--navy); margin: 8px 0 12px; }
    .hub__lede { color: #2d3748; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; }
    .hub__toc { background: #f0f4fa; border-left: 4px solid var(--navy); padding: 14px 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 4px; }
    .hub__toc-title { margin: 0 0 6px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 0.95rem; }
    .hub__toc ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 22px; line-height: 1.9; font-size: 0.92rem; }
    section { margin: 36px 0; scroll-margin-top: 20px; }
    section h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 12px; border-left: 5px solid var(--navy); padding-left: 12px; }
    section p { line-height: 1.9; color: #2d3748; margin: 0 0 14px; }
    section ul { line-height: 1.9; color: #2d3748; padding-left: 22px; }
    section a { color: var(--link); font-weight: 500; }
    .hub__articles { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 16px 0; }
    .hub__articles li { margin: 12px 0; padding: 12px 14px; background: #f7fafc; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; }
    .hub__articles a { text-decoration: none; display: block; }
    .hub__articles strong { color: var(--navy); font-size: 1rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px; }
    .hub__articles p { font-size: 0.88rem; color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; }
    .hub__cta { text-align: center; margin-top: 24px; font-weight: 600; }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
